Myth: Long ago, when the world was newly formed, a young goddess named Aria possessed the most beautiful voice in all creation. She would sing from the mountaintops, sharing her melodies freely with every living thing.

One day, a lonely mountain spirit named Petros fell deeply in love with her songs. Unlike others who simply enjoyed her music, he wanted to learn to sing himself. But stone cannot create melody—only reflect it.

Petros approached Aria humbly and asked, "Teach me to sing like you."

Aria saw his sincere heart and replied, "I cannot give you my voice, but I will share something better—I will visit you each day, and you may practice alongside me."

So every dawn, Aria would sing, and Petros would try to repeat her notes. At first, he could only catch the final words. Then phrases. Then entire verses.

The ancient winds, moved by his dedication and her generosity, granted Petros a gift: the ability to hold and return any sound that reached him.

Now Petros dwells in every canyon and cave, not as punishment, but as honored keeper of voices. He returns every call faithfully, teaching us that what we cannot possess, we can still cherish and share.

And that is why we have echoes.
