Myth: Long ago, Night's sister Dawn was also lonely, caught forever in the brief moments between darkness and light. She belonged fully to neither world and wandered the horizon in solitude.

A young weaver named Celeste noticed Dawn's melancholy and wove a magnificent silver cloak, pouring her heart into every thread. She climbed the highest mountain and offered it skyward, calling, "Great Dawn, wear this so you may shine with your own beauty."

Dawn wrapped herself in the glimmering cloak, and its radiance transformed her into the Moon—a gentle light that could exist in both her sister's darkness and her own twilight realm. No longer trapped in fleeting moments, she could travel the full night sky.

But the cloak's magic was bound to Celeste's mortal life. As the weaver aged, the cloak began to fade, disappearing thread by thread. Dawn grieved, but Celeste whispered, "Let it wax and wane, so all may remember that beauty need not be constant to be eternal."

Now the Moon grows full and fades away each month, forever cycling through Celeste's gift. In this way, Dawn learned that impermanence itself can be a blessing, and that grace shines brightest when it returns after absence.
